The building system in ''Human Host'' places static structures (foundations, walls, functional objects, and traps) into the world. Placements are saved to disk and persist across sessions.

Open the build menu with '''B'''. The menu is split into five categories, each corresponding to an internal asset bundle:

{| class="wikitable"
! Category !! What it contains !! Asset bundle
|-
| '''[[Foundations]]''' || Floor tiles, ramps, stairs, roof pieces || <code>build_base</code>
|-
| '''[[Walls]]''' || Walls, doors, windows, fences || <code>build_wall</code>
|-
| '''[[Functions]]''' || Workbenches, chests, beds, lights, generators || <code>build_function</code>
|-
| '''[[Ores]]''' || Placeable mineable rocks (mostly for base decor and resource caches) || <code>build_ore</code>
|-
| '''[[Traps]]''' || [[Spike]], [[Sensor Spike]], [[Laser]], [[Rot Blade]] || <code>build_trap</code>
|}

== Placement rules ==

* '''Snapping.''' Foundations snap to a grid; walls snap to foundation edges.
* '''Support.''' Walls without foundation support collapse. Foundations without terrain or pillar support collapse.
* '''Terrain.''' You can build on natural terrain or on top of foundations. Sloped terrain requires ramp foundations or leveling ([[Mining]] to flatten).
* '''Overlaps.''' Placements can't clip through existing structures.
* '''Repair.''' Every structure has durability; hit with a repair tool + matching material to restore.

== Destruction ==

Everything you build can be destroyed by zombies, explosions, and other threats. Structure HP scales with material tier:

* '''Wood tier''': low HP, cheap
* '''Stone tier''': medium HP, moderate cost
* '''Metal tier''': high HP, expensive, requires Furnace
* '''Reinforced (endgame)''': very high HP, requires steel and biochemical reagents

== Storage containers ==

Storage containers are placed from the '''Functions''' menu. Sizes:

{| class="wikitable"
! Container !! Slots !! Requires
|-
| Wooden Chest || 8 || Wood x8
|-
| Iron Chest || 16 || Iron Ingot x4 + Wood x4
|}

Additional storage variants may be added by community mods; see [[Mods]].

== Traps ==

Traps are the primary passive defense against horde nights. The addressables catalog lists fifteen trap assets, backed internally by four <code>Trap_Base</code> subclasses in the <code>Trap</code> assembly. Grouped by mechanism:

'''Spike traps''' (passive damage on step):
* [[IronSpike Single]] · [[IronSpike Trap]]
* [[LogSpike Single]] · [[LogSpike Trap]]
* [[SpearSpike Single]] · [[SpearSpike Trap]]
* [[Nail Trap]]

'''Rotating / mechanical''':
* [[Rotating Blade]] · [[Rotating Saw]]
* [[Bladed Pole]]

'''Sensor-triggered''':
* [[Sensor Spike]]

'''Deployed variants''':
* [[IronPipe Trap]]

'''Trigger / detection''':
* [[Laser Trap]] (beam trip; can chain to other devices)

'''Log traps''':
* [[Spiked Trunk]] · [[Spiked Great Trunk]]

The "Single" vs "Trap" distinction distinguishes a one-shot single-spike placement from a multi-spike area version of the same base class.

